(window.MUSEUM_MATCH_T = window.MUSEUM_MATCH_T || {})["en"] = {"ui":{"lead":"Great games, one move at a time. Follow famous games on the board while Musowl explains each move.","all":"All","k_chess":"Chess","k_shogi":"Shogi","k_go":"Go","k_xiangqi":"Xiangqi","side_r":"Red","res_r":"Red won","back":"Back to Great Games","start":"Start","prev":"Back one move","next":"Forward one move","end":"End","play":"Play","pause":"Pause","moves":"Moves","move":"Move {n}","startpos":"Start","nextnote":"Next note (move {n})","quiet":"No note for this move.","guide":"Musowl","keys":"← → step through the moves","result":"Result","r_event":"Game","r_date":"Year","r_place":"Place","side_w":"White","side_b":"Black","side_s":"Sente","side_g":"Gote","side_B":"Black","side_W":"White","res_w":"White won","res_b":"Black won","res_d":"Draw","res_s":"Sente won","res_g":"Gote won","credit":"The moves are the public record of each game. The notes are by the museum’s Musowl."},"opera":{"t":"The Opera Game","w":"Paul Morphy","b":"Duke Karl II of Brunswick and Count Isouard","ev":"Played in a box at the Paris Opera (the two allies consulting)","d":"1858","pl":"France (Paris)","lead":"The young American champion against two noblemen, during a performance at the opera: seventeen moves. Bring the pieces out fast, give them up without a second thought, and finish with very few. No game appears in more chess books.","c":{"0":"Paris, 1858. Twenty-one-year-old American Paul Morphy had been beating Europe's strong players one after another. That evening, in a box at the opera, the Duke of Brunswick and Count Isouard took Black together and played him, talking over each move. It is said Morphy wanted to finish quickly so he could watch the stage. These seventeen moves are full of the basics: bring pieces out fast, keep your king safe.","1":"White pushes the king's pawn two squares. It eyes d5 and e5 in the centre and opens paths for the bishop and queen.","2":"Black also plays e5. The pawns face each other in the centre, and White's pawn cannot go further.","3":"The knight comes to f3 and attacks the e5 pawn. A good move: it develops a piece and makes Black think about defending.","4":"Black guards e5 with d6. This is called the Philidor Defence. It is solid, but it makes the path of the bishop on f8 a little narrow.","5":"White plays d4, bringing another pawn to the centre to hit e5. Black must decide whether to take or defend.","6":"Black's bishop goes to g4. It presses the knight on f3, which stands in line with White's queen on d1. If the knight moves, the queen can be taken, so the knight cannot go after e5.","7":"White takes on e5 with the pawn. If Black took back at once with the d6 pawn, White would trade queens and then the knight would grab the e5 pawn. So Black first wants to get rid of the knight on f3.","8":"Black takes the knight with the bishop. Now e5 can be won back safely. But giving up the bishop that had just come out for the knight leaves Black behind in development.","9":"White takes back with the queen. White's queen is already working near the middle, while Black has hardly brought out any pieces.","10":"Black takes back with a pawn, and the material is even. But White is ahead in pieces that are out and ready.","11":"Bishop to c4. Together with the queen on f3 it aims at f7. Only the king guards f7, so it is a weak square; taking it would be mate.","12":"Black brings the knight to f6 and blocks the f-file. Without this, queen takes f7 would be mate. Defending while developing is the right answer.","13":"The queen swings to b3 and attacks f7 and b7 at the same time. A move with two threats is strong, because the opponent cannot defend both.","14":"Black plays the queen to e7 to guard f7, letting b7 go. But the queen now blocks the bishop on f8.","15":"Morphy does not take the pawn on b7; he develops a knight instead. Taking would send the queen far away. Choosing fast development over a pawn is typical of Morphy.","16":"Black plays c6, covering d5 and b5 and opening a road for the queen to b6.","17":"Bishop to g5 pins the knight on f6. If the knight moves, the queen on e7 behind it can be taken. Black's pieces find it harder and harder to move.","18":"Black plays b5 to chase the bishop from c4. It also gains room on the queen's side, but it opens a gap for White's pieces to jump in.","19":"The knight is given up to take on b5! Instead of retreating the bishop, Morphy keeps attacking. With Black's king still in the middle, this is very dangerous for Black.","20":"Black takes the knight and is a knight ahead. But Black's king is still on e8, and White's pieces keep gathering.","21":"Bishop to b5, check! Black's king is still stuck in the middle. White has two pawns for the knight and comes straight at the king.","22":"Black blocks with the knight on d7. But this knight is pinned: if it moves, the black king is in check. White will now aim at d7.","23":"White castles queenside. The king is safe, and at the same time the rook arrives on d1 to aim at d7. One move does both defence and attack: a superb move.","24":"Black brings the rook to d8 to guard d7. Black still cannot castle, and the rook on h8 and bishop on f8 are doing nothing.","25":"The rook is given up to take on d7! White hands over one rook to remove one of Black's defenders.","26":"Black takes back with the rook. Now this rook on d7 is pinned too: if it moves, the bishop on b5 checks the black king.","27":"The other rook comes to d1 and attacks d7 again. Now more white pieces attack d7 than black pieces defend it.","28":"Black's queen goes to e6, offering a queen trade to push White's queen away from b3, and hoping to help d7. But it is too late.","29":"The bishop takes on d7 with check. White removes Black's defenders one by one.","30":"Black takes back with the knight. With the knight gone from f6, the diagonal from the bishop on g5 to d8 is open. That diagonal will matter at the very end.","31":"The queen is given up on b8, with check! This is the most famous move of the game. Black must take with the knight, and when the knight leaves d7 the d-file opens.","32":"Black takes with the knight. Now no black piece guards d8.","33":"Rook to d8, checkmate! The bishop on g5 supports d8, and the black king is boxed in by its own pieces. White gave away queen, rook and bishop, and finished with just a rook and a bishop. Develop quickly, keep your king safe, and attack while the enemy king is stuck in the middle: this famous game shows all these important ideas."}},"reti1910":{"t":"Réti's Mate","w":"Richard Réti","b":"Savielly Tartakower","ev":"A casual game (not a tournament)","d":"1910","pl":"Austria (Vienna)","lead":"Two future grandmasters, a casual game in Vienna, eleven moves. White gives up the queen and finishes with a double check: two pieces checking at once. The mating pattern is named after White: Réti's Mate.","c":{"0":"Vienna, 1910. White is twenty-year-old Richard Réti; Black is Savielly Tartakower. Both went on to become top players and are known for bringing new ideas to chess. This was not a tournament game but a casual one, played for a small stake. It is short, but it shows clearly why you should get your king safe early.","1":"White pushes the king's pawn two squares. Taking the middle of the board makes it easier for the pieces behind to come out. The bishop and queen now have open paths.","2":"Black plays c6. Instead of going to the centre at once, Black gets ready for d5 to hit the pawn on e4. This solid opening is called the Caro-Kann Defence.","3":"White puts the other pawn on d4 too. Two pawns stand side by side in the centre, and White has plenty of space. Black will try to break them up.","4":"As planned, d5. The pawn challenges e4: \"I can take you.\" White must decide whether to guard it, push it or trade it.","5":"White brings out a knight to guard e4. It develops a piece and defends at the same time, so no time is wasted.","6":"Black takes the pawn on e4. By trading centre pawns, Black makes White's centre smaller.","7":"White takes back with the knight, which now stands in the middle of the board. A central knight reaches many squares and is a strong piece.","8":"Black brings out a knight to face the one on e4. Bishop to f5 is often played here, but Black invites a knight trade.","9":"The queen comes out early to d3 to guard the knight on e4. Usually you should not bring the queen out too soon, but White has a plan. Remember that this queen stands on the d-file, in front of Black's king.","10":"Black plays e5, offering a pawn to open lines for the bishop and queen. It is an active move that starts a fight in the centre.","11":"White takes the pawn on e5. White accepts the gift, but this also gives Black's queen more room to move.","12":"Check! Black's queen on a5 aims diagonally at the king on e1. At the same time it plans to win back the e5 pawn: two jobs in one move. White must block.","13":"White blocks the check with the bishop on d2. The queen could take the bishop, but White's queen guards it well.","14":"Black wins the pawn back, so material is equal again. And this queen looks straight down the e-file at the knight on e4 and the king behind it. If the knight moves, the king is in check, so the knight is stuck. Black seems about to win it.","15":"White castles queenside. The king leaves the e-file, so the knight is free again. At the same time a rook comes to d1: the rook on d1, the queen on d3 and the square d8 next to Black's king are all on one line. This is White's trap.","16":"Black takes the knight on e4 and seems to win a piece. But Black's king is still on e8 and has not castled. White's pieces are lined up on the d-file, and the black king is unprotected. This move, which loses the game, is the turning point.","17":"Queen to d8, check! White gives away the strongest piece for nothing. The only black piece that can take it is the king. By letting it be taken, White drags the king onto the dangerous square d8.","18":"Black must take with the king; there is no other way to stop the check. The king is pulled out to d8, with the rook on d1 waiting behind.","19":"The bishop moves from d2 to g5 with check, and at the same time it opens the way for the rook on d1, which checks along the d-file too. Two pieces checking at once is a double check. You cannot stop it by taking one piece or by blocking, so the king must run.","20":"The king runs to c7. If it went to e8 instead, the rook would come to d8 and mate. Wherever the king goes, White has a finishing move ready.","21":"Bishop to d8: checkmate on the diagonal against the king on c7! The bishop is guarded by the rook on d1, so the king cannot take it. Squares like b8, c8, b7 and c6 are blocked by Black's own pieces, and the rook watches d6 and d7. In just eleven moves, White gave up the queen and won with two small pieces. The game shows how dangerous it is to grab material before castling. Some records say Black resigned one move earlier."}},"immortal":{"t":"The Immortal Game","w":"Adolf Anderssen","b":"Lionel Kieseritzky","ev":"An informal game during a break in the London 1851 tournament","d":"1851","pl":"United Kingdom (London)","lead":"London, during the first international chess tournament: a game played in a break. White gives up a bishop, both rooks and finally the queen, and mates with the three small pieces left. Black is said to have resigned before the end; the last few moves may be the finish White showed.","c":{"0":"London, 1851. During a break in the tournament, Anderssen of Germany (White) and Kieseritzky, a star of Paris (Black), played a friendly game.","1":"White pushes the king's pawn two squares.","2":"Black does the same.","3":"f4! White offers a pawn: the King's Gambit. The idea is to pull Black's e5 pawn away from the centre.","4":"Black takes the pawn.","5":"The bishop comes out and eyes f7, next to Black's king.","6":"Check! With White's f-pawn gone, there is an open diagonal from h4 to e1.","7":"The king steps aside. Now White can never castle.","8":"Black offers a pawn too, to lure the bishop to b5.","9":"White takes it.","10":"A knight comes out and attacks the e4 pawn.","11":"White brings out a knight and chases the queen from h4.","12":"The queen drops back to h6.","13":"d3 guards e4 and opens the way for the bishop on c1.","14":"Knight to h5. The plan is to jump to g3 with check and hit the rook on h1.","15":"White's knight goes to h4, ready to jump to f5 and bother Black's queen.","16":"Black's queen goes to g5 and attacks the knight on h4.","17":"Knight to f5, a fine square guarded by the e4 pawn. It also eyes the pawn on g7.","18":"c6 tries to chase away the bishop on b5.","19":"White does not save the bishop. g4 chases the knight on h5 instead.","20":"The knight goes back to f6.","21":"Rook to g1, guarding the g4 pawn. White is happy to let the bishop on b5 go. A bold move!","22":"Black takes the bishop and is a whole piece ahead.","23":"The h-pawn starts chasing Black's queen.","24":"The queen goes to g6.","25":"h5 chases the queen again.","26":"The queen can only go back to g5. It has fewer and fewer squares.","27":"White's queen goes to f3. If the bishop then takes on f4, Black's queen may be trapped.","28":"Black brings the knight home to g8, so the queen can escape to f6.","29":"The bishop takes the f4 pawn and hits the queen.","30":"The queen escapes to f6.","31":"A knight comes out, planning to jump to d5 and hit the queen.","32":"The bishop comes out and aims at the rook on g1.","33":"Knight to d5, attacking the queen on f6 and the square c7. White doesn't mind losing the rook on g1.","34":"The queen grabs the b2 pawn and attacks the rook on a1.","35":"Bishop to d6! White lets both rooks go. In return, the pieces build a net around Black's king.","36":"Black takes the rook on g1.","37":"e5! This pawn blocks the diagonal from the queen on b2 to g7. Black's queen can no longer come back to help the king.","38":"Black takes the other rook too, with check.","39":"The king goes to e2. White has only the queen, two knights and one bishop left.","40":"The knight goes to a6 to guard c7. Black is said to have resigned around here; the rest may be the finish White showed.","41":"The knight takes on g7 with check.","42":"The king runs to d8.","43":"The queen is given up on f6, with check! The last big piece goes too.","44":"Black must take with the knight.","45":"Bishop to e7, checkmate! The knight on d5 covers e7 and c7, and the knight on g7 covers e8. White wins with just a bishop and two knights."}},"mimiaka":{"t":"The Ear-reddening Game","w":"Honinbo Shusaku","b":"Inoue Gennan Inseki","ev":"A game in Osaka (played over three days)","d":"1846","pl":"Japan (Osaka)","lead":"Seventeen-year-old Shusaku (4-dan) took on Gennan (8-dan), one of the strongest players of the day. When Shusaku played move 127, Gennan’s ears are said to have turned red — hence the name. There was no komi then; Black won by 2 points.","c":{"0":"Osaka, 1846. Black is 17-year-old Shusaku (4-dan); White is Inoue Gennan Inseki (8-dan), about 48, one of the top players of the day who had once fought Honinbo Jowa for the post of Meijin Godokoro. The day before, Shusaku had started with two handicap stones, as their ranks required, but Gennan soon saw his strength and stopped that game; this one was played with Shusaku simply taking Black. There was no komi in those days, so only the territory on the board counts. Let us follow this three-day game move by move.","1":"Black 1 is on the 3-4 point at top right (R16). It balances corner territory and room to extend along the sides. Shusaku liked 3-4 points when he had Black.","2":"White 2 takes the 3-4 point at top left (D17). In the opening the corners come first, because territory is easiest to make there. The two sides share out the empty corners.","3":"Black 3 is the 3-4 point at bottom right (Q3). It is lined up with R16 so that the right side between the two corners can later become a big Black area.","4":"White 4 (P17) approaches Black’s top-right corner. If Black got one more move there, the corner would become very solid, so White acts first. Black can answer, or play another big point instead.","5":"Black 5 does not answer but takes the 3-4 point at bottom left (C4). Three 3-4 points at moves 1, 3 and 5 is a pattern often seen in Shusaku’s games with Black. Dealing with White’s P17 can wait.","6":"White 6 (C14) is on the left side. Approaching Black’s bottom-left corner would be normal, but Gennan builds on the left side together with D17. It is a slightly unusual move that blocks Black’s C4 from spreading up the left side.","7":"Black 7 (E4) closes the bottom-left corner with a second stone. The corner is now almost surely Black’s. By defending before White could approach, Black also limits what White 6 can do on the left.","8":"White 8 (R5) approaches Black’s Q3. With black 3-4 points at both ends of the right side, White needs a foothold there. A long fight in the bottom right begins.","9":"Black 9 (Q15) defends the top right. Placed below White’s P17, it strengthens the corner and pushes P17 towards the narrow top edge. Black wants to keep the right side on its side.","10":"White 10 (O4) is the taisha: instead of a simple move, White caps Black’s stones diagonally from a little distance. It has so many variations that an old saying speaks of “the taisha’s hundred changes”, and it was Gennan’s speciality. How will 17-year-old Shusaku handle his opponent’s favourite weapon?","11":"With 11 (Q5) Black attaches to White’s R5 and accepts the fight. A quiet defence was possible, but Shusaku chooses the line with many variations. A reading contest follows.","12":"White 12 (Q4) wedges between Black’s stones, trying to separate Q3 from Q5 and weaken one of them.","15":"With 13 (P4) and 15 (P3), Black firmly connects the lower stones. White 14 (R4) dug into the corner, so the corner territory is now contested. Black prefers to stay connected and strong even if it gives up a little of the corner.","17":"With White 16 (P5) and Black 17 (Q6), the stones grow upwards. Black pokes its head out towards the right side, while White presses from above to keep Black in.","19":"White 18 (R3) adds a stone in the corner to make life, and Black 19 (O3) holds White back on the bottom side. Black’s plan: let White have the corner, but become strong outside along the bottom and right.","20":"White 20 (P6) caps Black’s Q6 from above, trying to shut Black low on the right side. If Black cannot get out upwards, its right-side stones will only just live, with little to gain.","21":"With 21 (Q7) and 23 (Q8), Black crawls up the right side looking for a way out, while White 22 (P7) presses alongside. Black’s long chain on the third line is low and makes little territory, but it is clearly alive.","24":"White 24 (N4) seals Black’s O3–P3 stones on the bottom from outside. Black’s stones now face two directions, the right side and the bottom, and protecting both at once is getting hard.","25":"Black 25 (N3) extends one step along the bottom. Later study says another move here, one using a ladder, would have been better, so this is called Black’s small mistake in the game. Black’s bottom stones stay low and easy to surround, which gives White a chance to strike back. Even Shusaku could go astray inside his opponent’s favourite pattern.","26":"White 26 (P8) caps Black’s right-side stones from above. Black cannot get out upwards on the right, and is in danger of being shut in on the bottom too.","28":"White 28 (M3) attaches directly to Black’s N3 — a severe move. It stops Black from spreading along the bottom and takes away living space, while making Black add more stones. Gennan punished Black’s small slip at 25 at once.","30":"After Black 29 (M2), White 30 (L2) goes round underneath as well. Black’s bottom stones can get out neither outwards nor sideways. To live they must connect to the bottom-right corner, and a capturing fight over the corner begins.","31":"Black 31 (M4) is an important move. Instead of rushing into the corner fight, Black first creates a point White must answer. It prepares for the ko that is sure to come.","33":"With 33 (R2) the capturing race in the bottom-right corner begins. White 32 (L3) made White safe on the bottom, so Black must go for White’s corner stones. If Black captures them, its bottom stones live; if not, they are in danger.","35":"White 34 (S2) and Black 35 (S1): fine moves on the first and second lines in the corner. Each side reduces the other’s liberties (empty points around a group), trying to capture first.","36":"White 36 (N2). Before starting the ko, White first plays one move around Black’s bottom stones. Even if Black wins the ko, it will gain a little less — a well-timed move.","40":"White 40 (R1) captures a stone, and at last it is a ko. In a ko the side that was just captured may not retake at once. Black must first play a threat elsewhere that White has to answer, and only then retake. The fight is decided by who has more threats.","41":"Black 41 (O5) is the first ko threat. Ignoring it would put White’s bottom-right stones in trouble, so White answers with 42 (N5), and Black retakes with 43 (R2). It costs a little, but it is necessary here.","44":"White 44 (S7) is White’s ko threat. Played beside Black’s right-side stones, it makes Black answer at 45 (S8), and White retakes at 46 (R1). Each threat settles a little more of the board.","47":"Black 47 (M5), White 48 (N6), Black 49 (R2): another threat, another retake. Around White’s bottom stones, Black is slowly adding stones on the outside.","50":"White 50 (R7) cuts into Black’s right-side stones — a big ko threat. Normally Black would answer, but Black puts the ko first. It becomes a large trade: White gains on the right side, Black gets the bottom-right corner.","51":"Black 51 (P2) captures a stone and wins the ko. The bottom-right corner becomes living Black territory, and the bottom stones are saved. In return White continues with 52 (R8) to split Black’s right-side stones in two.","53":"Black 53 (R10) is an inventive move few would find. It comes to the aid of the upper part of Black’s split right-side stones from beyond White’s stones. Had Black answered simply, White would have taken a big share of the right side.","56":"White 54 (S10), Black 55 (S11), White 56 (S9): White makes life along the right edge, while Black holds it from above and keeps the upper half of the right side.","59":"After Black 57 (M6) and White 58 (N7), Black 59 (R11) connects the right-side stones firmly. Black’s right side is now safe from top to bottom. The big trade so far looks roughly even.","60":"White 60 (S3) is a big move. It reduces Black’s corner territory while strengthening White’s stones, and it also lets White play 62 (M1) next as a move Black must answer.","63":"White 62 (M1) captures a stone and Black 63 (O1) connects: the long bottom-right fight is over for now. White has territory on the bottom; Black has a thick wall on the right. Partly because of Black 25, White is thought to have done well so far. But Shusaku’s thick wall will later work hard in the centre.","65":"White 64 (G3) extends to the left part of the bottom to make White’s bottom area even bigger. Black 65 (G5) caps it from above so White’s territory cannot grow upwards. The pattern becomes clear: White takes territory, Black takes outside strength.","66":"White 66 (C9) is on the left side. Together with White 6 (C14), it builds a White framework there too. White is gathering territory along the bottom and the left.","67":"Black 67 (L17) is on the top side. With the thick wall behind it, Black aims for a large framework — a wide area likely to become territory — from the top towards the centre. It also pincers and attacks White’s P17.","69":"White 68 (J17) goes to the left of L17 to split Black’s top framework. Black 69 (Q17) attaches to White’s P17, attacking it while strengthening the corner.","70":"White 70 (O10) goes between Black’s right-side wall and the top framework. It stops Black’s framework from joining up through the centre and tries to link with White’s stones on the right.","72":"White 72 (P13) is a superb move. Placed just above Black’s wall, it makes a White home right in the middle of Black’s framework. Even if Black chases it, White can escape lightly. Black’s hopes for the top shrink with this one move.","73":"Black 73 (P12) blocks below P13 so it cannot connect to White’s right-side stones. White 74 (Q18) then goes into the top-right corner to trim Black’s corner territory as well.","77":"After Black 75 (R18) defends the corner, White 76 (O13) and Black 77 (N11) follow. Black caps White’s centre stones, trying to gain territory while attacking. From here to around move 100, Gennan does not make a single slack move and keeps dodging Black’s attacks.","79":"White 78 (N10) connects White’s centre stones, and Black 79 (O16) firms up the top. Black did not gain much by attacking, but holds the top territory securely.","80":"With 80 (Q12) and 82 (Q13), White links stones right beside Black’s wall. Now that White has a sound shape inside Black’s framework, Black’s top-right territory will be smaller than hoped.","83":"Black 83 (H3) goes into White’s bottom territory, which had grown too large. White 84 (H2) and Black 85 (J2) follow: a close fight over whether Black can live inside or escape outside.","87":"White 86 (J3), Black 87 (H4), White 88 (K2): Black links the invading stones to G5 above and brings them out. White’s bottom territory is trimmed, and Black adds to its central thickness.","89":"Black 89 (F17) approaches White’s D17 at top left, putting a black stone on the left of the top side and stopping White from spreading along it. White 90 (M17) and 92 (N17) try to make White’s stones live between Black’s on the top.","93":"With 93 (M18) and 95 (N16), Black squeezes White’s top stones from above and below. White can only live low on the first and second lines, and even then makes very little territory.","96":"From White 96 (R14) a sharp little fight starts beside Black’s top-right corner. White splits Black’s wall from the corner to weaken Black’s stones. Black answers at 97 (Q14), and White pushes along the right edge with 98 (R12) and 100 (S12).","101":"With 101 (T12) and 103 (T13), Black descends to the first line at the right edge, narrowing White’s living space. Edge moves look small, but here they decide life and death.","104":"White 104 (Q10) hits a weak spot in Black’s right-side wall. After Black 105 (Q11) and White 106 (P11), Black goes to capture with 107.","107":"Black 107 (P10) captures a white stone. Black’s right-side wall is now solidly connected and safe. White 108 (O11) and 110 (P9) head for the centre to protect White’s stones near the wall.","112":"From White 112 (L18), White’s top stones hurry to make life. With 113 (K18), 115 (K17) and 117 (K19), Black surrounds them so they cannot get out.","118":"White 118 (M19) captures a stone and White’s top group lives. Living inside Black’s top framework cuts Black’s territory a lot. Gennan’s play so far has been excellent.","120":"Black 119 (D18) probes White’s top-left corner and White answers with 120 (C17). Black is not trying to take the corner, but to firm up the left part of the top and create a chance to add territory.","122":"Black 121 (F15) tidies Black’s shape from the top towards the centre. White 122 (C6) plays beside Black’s bottom-left corner to trim its territory bit by bit.","124":"Black 123 (G2), White 124 (J1), Black 125 (F3): the border on the left of the bottom is settled. White captures a stone there and keeps its territory safe.","126":"White 126 (J5). White is now thick on the bottom and ready to push into the centre. With territory on the bottom and left and a living group at the top right, White leads. Most of the professionals watching are said to have thought White was ahead. Black cannot catch up by ordinary play.","127":"This is the famous ear-reddening move, Black 127 (K11). This one stone in the middle of the board is said to work in four directions at once. Upwards it widens Black’s framework; downwards it stops White’s thickness from reaching the centre; to the right it supports Black’s wall; to the left it blocks White’s expansion. One move doing four jobs. From here the game, thought to favour White, slowly starts to tip towards Black.","128":"When move 127 was played, a doctor watching nearby noticed that Gennan’s ears had turned red. He knew little about go, but he is said to have read it as a sign that Gennan was shaken, and guessed that Black would win. While the professionals thought White was better, only the doctor’s eye caught the turning point. White 128 (E18) defends the top-left corner.","131":"After Black 129 (F18) firms up the left of the top, Black 131 (D10) moves into White’s left-side framework. With stone 127 holding the centre, Black can safely invade White’s territory.","136":"With 133 (E10) and 135 (D12), Black’s left-side stones slip between White’s and take shape. White 136 (J10) comes into the centre to weaken the effect of stone 127; White cannot leave the centre to Black.","140":"Black 137 (H7) caps White’s bottom stones, and White 138 (G10) and 140 (F8) try to link White’s stones on the left of the centre. In the centre the stones are tangled, and both sides have weaknesses.","143":"White 142 (E2) slips into Black’s bottom-left corner. Black 143 (D7) and 145 (D6) connect Black’s stones while eyeing both White’s left-side stones and the white invader in the corner.","147":"Black 147 (K10) extends straight down from stone 127, cutting White’s centre stones into left and right. Here the single stone of 127 begins to show its real power.","150":"White 148 (K9), Black 149 (L9), White 150 (J11): White tries to connect its centre stones, Black cuts them off from the bottom. White 150 lands right next to stone 127, pushing into Black’s centre too.","155":"From Black 151 (J9) to Black 155 (K12), Black squeezes White’s centre stones from both sides. Black 155 connects above stone 127, joining Black’s centre stones with the top. Black has taken the initiative in the centre.","160":"After White 156 (C5) and Black 157 (D5), White plays 158 (J12) and 160 (H8). White protects its centre stones while also eyeing a weakness in Black’s bottom-left stones. The game is still close; neither side can relax.","162":"Black 161 (B8) goes underneath White’s left-side stones. White 162 (D13) caps Black’s left-side stones from above to make them suffer.","167":"After strengthening the centre with 163 (G12) and 165 (J13), Black 167 (C2) is an accurate move defending the bottom-left corner. Had Black ignored it, White’s stone at 142 (E2) could have threatened the corner. Black removes the danger first and builds a hard-to-lose position.","169":"White 166 (E17) and 168 (F16) try to cut Black’s stones at the top left. Black answers with 169 (G16) and 171 (G17), connecting the top stones firmly.","175":"Black 175 (G4) captures a white stone. Black’s stones survive inside White’s bottom area, so White’s territory there is smaller than it first looked. The gap is slowly closing.","180":"White 176 (M11), Black 177 (L13), White 178 (J6), Black 179 (M10), White 180 (M13): on the right of the centre too, White puts stones into Black’s framework to reduce it. Black answers while gathering the whole centre into its own territory.","187":"From Black 183 (K13) to 187 (M14), Black presses down on the white stones in the centre and keeps them away from the top. White fights to live there, but Black’s wall is thick and the fight is hard for White.","195":"From White 190 (G14) to 194 (M9), White widens the escape routes of its centre stones. Black 195 (L10) cuts between White’s centre stones to break their shape.","199":"With Black 199 (K7) the centre fight calms down. White’s centre stones manage to live, but Black gained territory along the way and the game now leans towards Black. Next comes the endgame — fixing the borders — where one or two points decide everything.","208":"Black 203 (E19), 205 (F2), White 208 (R19): endgame moves along the edges are played from the biggest down. In the endgame it is important to play moves the opponent must answer first.","212":"With 212 (D19) and 216 (F19), White captures black stones at the top edge, and Black makes up for it elsewhere with 213 (G11) and 217 (G19). Neither side gives an inch.","225":"Black 225 (L6) captures a white stone; Black keeps trimming White’s territory in the middle of the bottom. The last big fight, a ko in the centre, is approaching.","228":"White 228 (N12) captures a stone and creates a ko in the centre. For more than 80 moves the sides will capture and recapture at M13 and N13. Each ko threat causes a small trade somewhere on the board. This long, long ko will decide the game.","236":"White 236 (T8) captures a stone in the middle of the ko. Some later analysts think that ending the ko here instead would have kept the margin smaller. In a long ko, which trades you choose decides the last point or two.","262":"With 262 (T1) and 266 (S4), White captures black stones in the bottom-right corner. Meanwhile Black has taken back elsewhere, and the ko goes on with gains and losses piling up on both sides.","275":"Black 275 (T14) captures two white stones; this trade at the top-right edge favours Black. Adding up the trades during the ko, Black is just keeping its small lead.","299":"Black 299 (J16) captures four white stones at once. White’s threat at the top is gone and Black’s top is safe. Black has come through the last big crisis of the ko.","311":"Black 311 (M13) finally fills the ko. The centre ko that lasted more than 80 moves is over. Through all the trades it caused, Black has kept the lead. Only small endgame moves remain.","321":"From Black 317 (N1) to 321 (M2), the last borders on the bottom are fixed. Both sides keep playing accurately to the end, trying to gain even one more point.","325":"The game ends after 325 moves, and the count shows Black ahead by 2 points. Black stumbled a little in Gennan’s favourite taisha early on, and White was thought to be better up to the middle game. Shusaku turned the tide with the single stone of move 127, came through the long ko and held on to a tiny lead. Gennan is said to have remarked later that Shusaku’s strength was no less than 7-dan. This game made Shusaku’s name, and he later became heir to the Honinbo house. The taisha in the opening, the ear-reddening move 127 and the long final ko — these three are the highlights of this great game."}},"evergreen":{"t":"The Evergreen Game","w":"Adolf Anderssen","b":"Jean Dufresne","ev":"A casual game (not a tournament)","d":"1852","pl":"Germany (probably Berlin)","lead":"Anderssen, of the Immortal Game, played this other masterpiece a year later. Just as Black threatens mate, White quietly moves one rook, then gives up the queen and finishes with a double check. It is called \"evergreen\" because it never fades.","c":{"0":"A game published in a magazine in 1852. White is Anderssen; Black is his friend Dufresne. It is thought to have been played in Berlin, but no record says so for certain.","1":"White pushes the king's pawn two squares.","2":"Black does the same.","3":"The knight attacks the e5 pawn.","4":"Black guards e5 with a knight.","5":"The bishop comes out and eyes Black's f7.","6":"Black brings out a bishop too.","7":"b4! A pawn offer called the Evans Gambit. While Black takes it, White builds up the centre.","8":"Black takes the pawn.","9":"c3 chases the bishop and gets ready for d4.","10":"The bishop drops back to a5.","11":"d4 claims the centre.","12":"Black takes the pawn.","13":"White does not take back but castles. The king's safety and active pieces come before pawns.","14":"Black's pawn pushes on to d3, deep inside White's camp.","15":"Queen to b3. Together with the bishop on c4 it aims at f7.","16":"Black guards f7 with the queen.","17":"e5 chases the queen.","18":"The queen goes to g6.","19":"Rook to e1, onto the e-file to back up the e5 pawn.","20":"Black brings out a knight to get ready to castle.","21":"Bishop to a3, aiming along the diagonal at the knight on e7. If that knight moves, the bishop will reach f8, so castling on the king's side gets hard for Black.","22":"Black offers a pawn to pull White's pieces away.","23":"The queen takes it.","24":"Rook to b8, chasing the queen along the b-file.","25":"The queen goes to a4.","26":"The bishop drops back to b6.","27":"A knight comes out. Almost all of White's pieces are in play.","28":"Black develops the other bishop.","29":"Knight to e4, eyeing f6 next.","30":"Black's queen goes to f5 and attacks the knight on e4.","31":"The bishop takes the pawn on d3 and guards the knight on e4.","32":"Queen to h5, aiming at the knight on f3.","33":"The knight is given up on f6, with check!","34":"Black takes with a pawn.","35":"White takes back with a pawn and hits the knight on e7. On the e-file the rook on e1 is watching e7 too.","36":"Rook to g8, aiming at g2. If the queen on h5 takes on f3, mate is close.","37":"A quiet rook move to d1! White's own king is in danger, but instead of defending, White lines up on d7. This is the move that made the game \"evergreen\".","38":"Black takes the knight on f3. Taking on g2 next would be mate.","39":"But White moves first. The rook takes the knight on e7, with check!","40":"Black takes back with the knight from c6. Now the diagonal from a4 to d7 is open.","41":"The queen is given up on d7, with check!","42":"Black must take with the king.","43":"The bishop moves to f5 with check, and it opens the d-file for the rook on d1 too: a double check.","44":"The king goes to e8. On c6 the bishop would mate on d7.","45":"Bishop to d7, check.","46":"The king goes to f8.","47":"The bishop from a3 takes the knight on e7: checkmate! The bishop on d7 covers e8, and the pawn on f6 covers e7 and g7."}},"lasker1912":{"t":"The King Hunt","w":"Edward Lasker","b":"George Alan Thomas","ev":"A quick casual game at the City of London Chess Club","d":"1912","pl":"United Kingdom (London)","lead":"A fast, casual game at a London club. White gives up the queen and chases Black's king all the way across the board, from g8 to g1. The opening moves are written in slightly different orders in different records, but the position after move 10 is the same.","c":{"0":"October 1912, the City of London Chess Club. White is Edward Lasker, a young engineer just arrived from Germany; Black is George Alan Thomas, later British champion. It was a fast, casual game. It is famous as a \"king hunt\": Black's king is chased from g8 all the way to g1 at the other end of the board. Lasker later moved to America and became well known for his chess books.","1":"White starts with d4, the queen's pawn. It covers e5 and c5 and opens the way for the bishop on c1.","2":"Black plays e6, building a solid position and preparing f5 or d5.","3":"Knight to f3. It covers e5 and helps guard the king's side: the most natural square.","4":"Black plays f5, controlling e4, like the Dutch Defence. But moving a pawn in front of the king makes Black's king a little weaker.","5":"The other knight comes to c3, getting ready for e4 to hit the f5 pawn.","6":"Black brings a knight to f6. It controls e4 even more and prepares to castle.","7":"Bishop to g5, aiming at the knight on f6. That knight is an important defender of Black's king, so White wants to remove it early.","8":"Black brings the bishop to e7 to guard the knight, and gets closer to castling.","9":"White trades the bishop for the knight. The knight that guarded Black's king is gone.","10":"Black takes back with the bishop. Now this bishop on f6 takes over the job of guarding the king's side.","11":"e4! White hits the f5 pawn and gains space in the centre. Lines open, and White's pieces can move more freely.","12":"Black takes the pawn on e4. If not, White would push e5 and drive Black's pieces back.","13":"The knight takes back and sits in the centre, eyeing the bishop on f6 and the squares g5 and d6.","14":"Black plays b6, preparing to bring the bishop to b7 to aim down the long diagonal at the knight on e4.","15":"Knight to e5, a strong central square that also eyes g4 and f7. Both white knights are now close to Black's king's side.","16":"Black castles. The king is on g8. But with the f-pawn gone, there are many gaps around the king.","17":"Bishop to d3. For now its own knight on e4 is in the way, but behind it lies the pawn on h7. The moment the knight on e4 moves, this bishop will aim straight at h7.","18":"Black's bishop goes to b7 and aims down the long diagonal at the knight on e4, making White's pieces less free.","19":"Queen to h5, aiming at the pawn on h7, which only Black's king defends. White's queen, bishop and knights all point at Black's king.","20":"Black puts the queen on e7. It guards the bishop on f6 and gets ready to go to c5, but Black misses White's idea. Moves like h6 or taking the knight on e5 were needed. This is the turning point.","21":"The queen is given up on h7, with check! White hands over the strongest piece for nothing. Lasker saw the whole path of chasing the king to mate.","22":"Black must take with the king. The king is pulled out to h7.","23":"The knight takes the bishop on f6 with check. At the same time e4 is empty, so the bishop on d3 also checks the king on h7: a double check. It cannot be blocked, so the king must run.","24":"The king goes to h6. On h8, the other knight would jump to g6 and mate. The king is pushed further and further forward.","25":"The other knight jumps from e5 to g4 with check. It attacks the king on h6 and drives it further forward.","26":"The king goes to g5, leaving its own camp and walking toward White's side.","27":"Check with the h-pawn! Even the pawns join the hunt. The king has to keep moving forward.","28":"The king goes to f4. It has crossed the middle of the board and is entering White's camp.","29":"Check again with the g-pawn. Two pawns now block the way back for the king.","30":"The king goes to f3, right in the middle of White's pieces.","31":"The bishop drops back from d3 to e2 with check. The king is pushed even deeper.","32":"The king goes to g2. White's own king on e1 is not far away.","33":"Rook to h2, check. At last the rook joins in.","34":"The king finally reaches g1. It started on g8 and has walked to the first row at the other end of the board.","35":"White's king steps from e1 to d2. This opens the way for the rook on a1, which now checks the king on g1 along the first row: checkmate! Castling queenside would also have mated, but Lasker moved the king. In a quick game, after giving up the queen, White chased the king for fourteen moves into the deepest corner of White's own camp. A very rare game."}},"toketsu":{"t":"The Blood-vomiting Game","w":"Akaboshi Intetsu","b":"Honinbo Jowa","ev":"Played at the residence of Lord Matsudaira of Suo (over four days)","d":"1835","pl":"Japan (Edo)","lead":"A game born of the struggle for Meijin Godokoro, the highest post in Japanese go. Inoue Gennan Inseki sent his pupil Akaboshi Intetsu to face the Meijin, Jowa. After four days of hard fighting Intetsu lost, and he is said to have coughed up blood at the board.","c":{"0":"1835. Black is Akaboshi Intetsu (7-dan), White the Meijin, Honinbo Jowa. Gennan sent his best pupil, planning to argue that a Meijin who lost to a 7-dan did not deserve the title.","1":"Black 1 on the 3-4 point at bottom left (C4). Intetsu starts calmly.","4":"After four moves, each corner has one stone: White at bottom right and top left, Black at bottom left and top right.","6":"White 6 (E3) already approaches Black’s bottom-left corner. Jowa is active from the start.","10":"White 10 (P4) closes the bottom-right corner with a second stone (an enclosure).","12":"White 12 (D6) presses down on Black’s bottom-left corner from above. The biggest fight of the game begins here.","13":"Black 13 (E4) attaches directly to a white stone. Black accepts the fight.","20":"White 20 (C3) slips under Black’s corner. It eats into Black’s corner territory while trying to make White’s own stones live.","28":"White 28 (C9) reaches far along the left side. It is often called an over-extension — a fierce move typical of Jowa.","33":"Black 33 (C2). This is said to be a new line that the Inoue house had studied in secret. It attacks White’s floating stones in the centre while swallowing White’s stones on the left side.","36":"White 36 (F3). White tries to hold Black in from outside at bottom left while connecting its own stones.","43":"Black 43 (E9). White’s stones on the left are in trouble. Up to here Intetsu has played well and is thought to have taken the lead.","45":"Black 45 (D11) extends up the left side, keeping White’s stones apart while strengthening its own.","50":"White 50 (C16). White leaves the bottom left for now and turns to the top-left corner.","53":"Black 53 (G17). Black moves into the top side so that White cannot widen the top left.","58":"White 58 (C14) spreads from the top-left corner along the side, drawing close to Black’s stones on the left.","60":"White 60 (G4) captures two black stones. White’s group at bottom left finally gets a little breathing room.","62":"White 62 (J17). The two sides now face off along the top too. There are weak stones all over the board — a difficult position.","66":"White 66 (B14). Around the top-left corner, White works to settle its own stones.","68":"White 68 (B11). The next few white moves, together with move 80, became famous as Jowa’s brilliant moves. They were so remarkable that people said ghosts had shown them to him.","70":"White 70 (E12). Instead of answering Black on the left side directly, White plays another key point first. Hidden possibilities left behind make this possible.","72":"White 72 (R13). White turns to the right side to stop Black’s top-right area from growing.","76":"White 76 (O14). White links the right side to the centre and prepares to split Black’s stones.","80":"White 80 (Q10) makes an “empty triangle”, a shape usually considered bad. Yet it became the base for splitting Black’s stones in two and attacking them. It is often shown as the classic example of a bad-looking but excellent move.","83":"Black 83 (R3) goes into White’s bottom-right corner. A fierce fight of captures and recaptures follows there.","91":"Black 91 (Q4) captures a white stone. The bottom-right corner matters a lot for Black.","98":"White 98 (M4). White wraps around Black’s stones on the bottom side and does not let them live easily.","106":"White 106 (S5) captures a black stone. White keeps the bottom-right corner, and Black’s stones must run outwards.","113":"Black 113 (K6). Black’s stones on the bottom look for life by heading into the centre.","120":"White 120 (N9) blocks the running black stones. In the centre it is now a race to see whose stones weaken first.","128":"White 128 (L11) presses Black’s centre stones from above as well. Black still cannot relax.","138":"White 138 (K8) captures a stone and a ko starts in the centre. For about 30 moves the two sides capture and recapture at K8 and L8.","152":"White 152 (E11) is a ko threat. During the ko White keeps finding moves elsewhere that Black must answer.","170":"White 170 (P15). The centre ko has settled, and White turns to the top right, aiming at a big black group that is not yet fully alive.","178":"White 178 (J14) jumps into the middle of the top area to split Black’s stones left and right. The splitting attack prepared since move 80 now begins in earnest.","193":"Black 193 (J11) captures a white stone. Black fights hard to connect and find life.","201":"Black 201 (R14) captures a stone and a new ko begins on the right side, with captures going back and forth at R14 and R15.","206":"White 206 (M13). Even during the ko, White slowly tightens the grip on Black’s centre stones.","222":"White 222 (L14) captures a stone. Black’s stones at the top remain split in two, and saving both is getting hard.","230":"White 230 (C13) hits Black’s weak spot on the left side as well. White’s stones are now solidly connected across the board.","237":"With 237 (F12) and 239 (E13), Black captures back on the top left. Even so, the overall gap does not shrink.","246":"After White 246 (L8), Intetsu resigned. As the stones were being cleared, he is said to have coughed up blood onto the board. He had probably been ill for some time, and he died not long afterwards. For Jowa, it is called the greatest win of his career."}},"deepblue1997":{"t":"Deep Blue Wins","w":"Deep Blue (IBM computer)","b":"Garry Kasparov","ev":"Game 6 of the rematch with IBM's Deep Blue (a six-game match)","d":"1997","pl":"United States (New York)","lead":"World champion Kasparov against Deep Blue, a chess computer built by IBM, in a six-game match. With the score level after five games, the last game was over in only 19 moves. It was the first time a computer beat the world champion in a full match of several games.","c":{"0":"New York, 11 May 1997. World champion Garry Kasparov against Deep Blue, a chess computer built by IBM, in a six-game match. Kasparov had won their match the year before, but in this rematch against the stronger Deep Blue the score was 2½–2½ after five games. In the last game, Deep Blue had White. The whole world watched to see whether a human or a computer was stronger.","1":"White pushes the king's pawn two squares, taking the centre and opening paths for the pieces.","2":"Kasparov plays c6, getting ready for d5 to hit e4: the Caro-Kann Defence. It is solid, and players often chose it against computers.","3":"White brings the other pawn to the centre too. Two pawns stand side by side.","4":"Black plays d5 and hits e4. The fight for the centre pawns begins.","5":"The knight goes to c3 and guards e4. A natural move: develop and defend.","6":"Black takes the pawn on e4, trading centre pawns to make White's centre smaller.","7":"The knight takes back. White's knight now stands in the middle of the board.","8":"Black puts a knight on d7. Then, when the other knight goes to f6, Black can take back with this knight if White trades knights.","9":"The knight jumps to g5, a sharp plan aiming at f7 and e6. White attacks early, before Black's king side is settled.","10":"Black brings the knight to f6. Now more pieces guard the king.","11":"Bishop to d3. It aims diagonally at h7 and points toward Black's king side.","12":"Black plays e6 to strengthen the defence and open the way for the bishop on f8. But the e6 pawn also becomes a target for White's knight.","13":"The other knight goes from g1 to f3. White is ready to castle.","14":"Black plays h6 to chase the knight on g5. But in this position a knight sacrifice on e6 was already known for White. People found it strange that a master like Kasparov chose this, and some think he mixed up the move order by accident. This is the turning point.","15":"The knight is given up on e6! In return, White breaks the pawns around Black's king and stops Black from castling. Deep Blue is said to have played this move from the opening moves it had stored in advance.","16":"Black doesn't take at once but puts the queen on e7. On the e-file it lines up on the knight on e6 and White's king behind it. If the knight moves, the king is in check, so the knight cannot escape.","17":"White castles and moves the king away. Now the knight on e6 is no longer pinned. White does not mind if the knight is taken.","18":"Black takes the knight and is ahead in material. But the pawns around Black's king are broken, and the king is poorly protected.","19":"Bishop to g6, check. With the f7 pawn gone, the diagonal reaches the king on e8.","20":"The king goes to d8. Black can no longer castle, and the king is stuck near the middle. Black's pieces get in each other's way around the king.","21":"The other bishop goes to f4, eyeing c7 and b8 and squeezing the space around Black's king even more.","22":"Black plays b5, making room on the queen's side to bring the bishop to b7 and give the king a way out.","23":"a4 hits the pawn on b5. White wants to open lines on the queen's side so the rook on a1 can join the attack.","24":"Black brings the bishop to b7, aiming down the long diagonal at g2. Black's pieces are finally starting to move.","25":"Rook to e1. On the e-file it eyes the e6 pawn and the queen on e7 behind it. All of White's pieces have joined the attack.","26":"Black's knight goes to d5, a strong central square, attacking the bishop on f4 and protecting e6.","27":"The bishop drops back to g3. It keeps aiming at c7 and b8, keeping the pressure on Black's king.","28":"Black's king goes to c8, a little safer, and lets the rook on d8 move.","29":"White takes the pawn on b5. The a-file opens, and the rook on a1 now eyes the pawn on a7.","30":"Black takes back with a pawn. But fewer and fewer pawns are left around Black's king.","31":"Queen to d3, eyeing the b5 pawn and the square f5. White's pieces slowly surround Black's king.","32":"Black brings the bishop back to c6 to guard the b5 pawn.","33":"Bishop to f5! It can be taken for free. But if a pawn takes it, the e-file pawn is gone, and the rook on e1 reaches the queen on e7 directly.","34":"Black takes with a pawn. Now nothing stands on the e-file between e1 and e7.","35":"The rook takes the queen on e7. White has given a knight, a bishop and a rook, but has won the strongest piece, the queen.","36":"Black takes back the rook with the bishop. Material is about even, but Black's king has almost no shelter.","37":"c4! The pawn hits both b5 and d5, and Black's defenders start to fall apart. White's queen is ready to join the attack at any moment. Kasparov resigned here, probably feeling his king was too open to stop the attack. Only 19 moves. It was the first time a computer beat the world champion in a full match of several games: a historic moment. After this, using computers to study and train became normal in chess."}},"seigen1933":{"t":"The 3-3, Star Point and Tengen Game","w":"Go Seigen","b":"Honinbo Shusai","ev":"Commemorative game with the Meijin (over three months)","d":"1933–1934","pl":"Japan (Tokyo)","lead":"Nineteen-year-old Go Seigen (5-dan) played the Meijin, Honinbo Shusai. With Black he opened on the 3-3 point, then a star point, then the very centre (tengen) — breaking every rule of the day and astonishing the go world. With many adjournments the game lasted over three months, and White won by 2 points.","c":{"0":"1933. That year Go Seigen and his friend Kitani Minoru were spreading a new style of opening, the “Shinfuseki”. Everyone wanted to see how he would use it against the Meijin.","1":"Black 1 is on the 3-3 point at top right (R17). It takes the corner in one move, but it has little influence outwards, so in those days nobody thought of it as an opening move.","3":"Black 3 is on the star point at bottom left (D4). Unlike the 3-3 point, the star point cares more about spreading outwards.","5":"Black 5 goes right to the centre of the board — tengen (K10). Not a corner, not a side, but the middle. Against the Meijin, Go Seigen put his new ideas on show head-on.","6":"3-3, star point, tengen: the three black stones each have a different job — corner territory, outward reach and central power. With 6 (Q16), White plays right next to Black’s 3-3 stone.","9":"Black 9 (P18). Black settles the top-right corner compactly and safely — a territory-first style that suits the 3-3 point.","11":"Black 11 (K4) goes to the middle of the bottom side, right below tengen. Working with the tengen stone, Black aims for a big area along the bottom.","12":"White 12 (M3) moves straight into the bottom side so that Black cannot build a big framework. The Meijin stays calm and takes Black’s plans apart one by one.","13":"Black 13 (D10), now in the middle of the left side. Black’s stones stand high on the board, forming a wide framework together.","14":"White 14 (E16) closes the top-left corner with a second stone (an enclosure). The Meijin is not thrown by Black’s new style and calmly secures the corner.","21":"Black 21 (G10) links tengen with the left-side stone, and Black’s influence spreads across the centre.","28":"White 28 (D3) slides under Black’s star-point stone at bottom left and takes the corner. Black lets White have it in exchange for strength outside.","30":"White 30 (Q12) extends along the right side, also putting a lid over Black’s 3-3 corner and weakening its reach.","37":"Black 37 (P15) captures a white stone. In the top right the two sides clash in a close fight.","40":"White 40 (O10) steps out from the right side towards the centre, approaching Black’s influence around tengen.","44":"White 44 (H17) takes a point on the top side to stop Black’s stones spreading upwards.","45":"Black 45 (R7) starts work on White’s lower-right position. A long fight follows from the right side down to the bottom right.","59":"Black 59 (R4) also gets into the bottom-right corner and reduces White’s territory.","70":"White 70 (N6) pushes from the bottom side into the centre, aiming to separate Black’s bottom-right stones from the centre.","79":"Black 79 (L9) firms up the centre around the tengen stone. So far the game looks fairly even.","80":"White 80 (C6) moves from the bottom-left corner up the left side, targeting Black’s stones there.","98":"White 98 (A11) captures two black stones. White lives neatly at the left edge, and Black’s left side turns out smaller than planned.","101":"Black 101 (S14) returns to the top right to shrink White’s position on the right side.","105":"Black 105 (K17) takes the middle of the top side and starts attacking White’s stones there.","117":"Black 117 (F17) also goes into the area around White’s top-left corner, and the whole top side becomes a battlefield.","124":"White 124 (K15) runs its top-side stones towards the centre, cutting between Black’s stones on the way.","135":"Black 135 (E9) captures two white stones. Black wins something back on the left side.","138":"White 138 (N18) secures territory on the top side. Over the whole board the position is very close.","153":"Black 153 (K14). Black’s centre stones form a thick road linking top and bottom. How White can break into that thickness is the key question.","159":"Black 159 (F4) defends the bottom left firmly. White’s next move is the most famous move of this game.","160":"White 160 (H8) leaps into a gap in Black’s thickness — a sharp move with several aims at once, from which White starts breaking into Black’s centre. A rumour spread that a pupil, not Shusai, had found it, but this has never been proved.","161":"Black 161 (J8) answers at once, but White has more than one aim.","162":"White 162 (K12) switches to the upper side of the centre and threatens the links between Black’s stones.","172":"White 172 (F13) links stones from the centre to the top left. Little by little, Black’s central thickness stops turning into territory.","178":"White 178 (N9) gets into Black’s area on the right of the centre too.","187":"With 187 (M6) and 189 (L7), Black captures white stones in reply. Even so, White has already got to the important points first.","198":"White 198 (E12) captures a stone. White’s top-left stones are connected too, and White is slightly ahead overall.","203":"Black 203 (B3). From here it is endgame: the borders along the edges are fixed point by point.","224":"White 224 (T10) captures a stone. Even at the right edge, White does not give away a single point.","233":"Black 233 (L13) captures two white stones. Black keeps chasing to the very end.","252":"The game ends after 252 moves: White wins by 2 points. With no komi Black had the small advantage of moving first, but the Meijin held steady to the end. Even so, Go Seigen’s 3-3, star point and tengen became a sensation and announced a new age of openings."}},"century1956":{"t":"The Game of the Century","w":"Donald Byrne","b":"Bobby Fischer","ev":"The third Rosenwald Trophy tournament (Rosenwald Memorial)","d":"1956","pl":"United States (New York)","lead":"Thirteen-year-old Bobby Fischer beats Donald Byrne, one of America's strongest masters. On move 17 Fischer lets his queen be taken for nothing, then wins piece after piece back, and at the end his small pieces hunt down White's king. Fischer later became world champion.","c":{"0":"New York, 17 October 1956, a tournament game. Black is thirteen-year-old Fischer; White is Byrne, one of the top American players.","1":"White starts with a knight.","4":"Fischer plays g6, getting ready to put his bishop on the long diagonal.","8":"Black castles, making the king safe first.","10":"Black plays d5. The fight for the centre begins.","15":"With e4, White has two pawns side by side in the centre.","17":"Rook to d1, supporting the pawn on d4. White has still not castled.","18":"The knight goes to b6 and chases the queen on c4.","20":"Bishop to g4, putting pressure on the knight on f3.","21":"White's bishop goes to g5. White's king is still in the middle.","22":"Knight to a4! It jumps to a square where it can be taken. The idea: if the knight on c3 goes away, the e4 pawn loses its guard.","23":"White doesn't take the knight and moves the queen to a3.","26":"Black's knight takes the e4 pawn. White's centre is breaking down.","27":"White's bishop takes the e7 pawn, attacking Black's queen and the rook on f8 at once.","28":"Queen to b6! Black lets the rook on f8 go. Fischer is counting on White's king still being in the middle.","29":"White doesn't take the rook but develops the bishop.","30":"The knight takes the c3 pawn and attacks the rook on d1.","31":"Bishop to c5, attacking Black's queen on b6.","32":"Check from the rook on the e-file!","33":"The king goes to f1. Now White can never castle.","34":"Bishop to e6! The most famous move of the game: Black lets the queen be taken for nothing. In return, Black has a plan to win back piece after piece.","35":"White takes Black's queen.","36":"Bishop to c4, check. Black's counter-attack begins.","38":"The knight checks from e2.","40":"When the king goes back to f1, the knight moves and uncovers a check from the bishop on c4. On the way, the knight grabs the pawn on d4.","44":"The knight returns to c3, another uncovered check, and now it attacks the rook on d1. Checking again and again while picking up pieces is called a \"windmill\".","46":"Black takes back the bishop. The knight on c3 is still attacking the rook on d1.","50":"The knight takes the rook on d1. Now Black has won back more than the queen and is clearly ahead.","52":"The rook takes the a2 pawn as well.","56":"Black trades rooks. The fewer pieces left, the better for the side with more.","57":"White's queen gives a check.","58":"Black blocks with the bishop.","64":"b5. Black's pawn starts to march.","68":"The king moves to g7 and frees the bishop on f8.","70":"Now Black's pieces hunt the white king. Check with a bishop.","72":"The knight checks on g3.","74":"Check with the other bishop.","76":"Bishop to b3, check.","78":"The knight checks on e2.","80":"The knight checks on c3. The king is chased around.","82":"Rook to c2: checkmate! Rook, two bishops and knight all work together. A thirteen-year-old showed how many pieces can play as one team."}},"oshiro1849":{"t":"Shusaku’s First Castle Game","w":"Honinbo Shusaku","b":"Yasui Sanchi IX","ev":"Castle Game (the yearly game at Edo Castle)","d":"1849","pl":"Japan (Edo)","lead":"The Castle Games were the yearly showcase where players of the official go houses played at the shogun’s castle. Twenty-year-old Shusaku made his debut this year and won with Black by 11 points — the start of a perfect record of 19 wins in 19 Castle Games.","c":{"0":"1849. The year before, Shusaku had been named heir to the Honinbo house, and now he plays his first Castle Game. It is said that such games were often played beforehand elsewhere and only replayed at the castle.","1":"Black 1 on the 3-4 point at top right (R16). As usual, Shusaku starts with a calm 3-4 point.","4":"White 4 (C15) approaches Black’s D17 in the top left. A fight starts there early on.","5":"Black 5 (C11) pincers the approaching white stone from below, so White cannot settle easily on the left side.","12":"White 12 (C17). White takes the top-left corner and Black gets stones facing outwards — a fair trade for both.","13":"Black 13 (J17) makes use of the outside stones by extending along the top.","15":"Black 15 (C5) takes the last empty corner, at bottom left. All four corners are now occupied.","16":"White 16 (E4) approaches that black stone at once, and a fight starts at bottom left too.","19":"Black 19 (D2) firmly secures the bottom-left corner — a solid, Shusaku-like choice.","23":"Black 23 (E5) comes out between White’s stones and strengthens Black’s group at bottom left.","29":"Black 29 (F11) reinforces Black’s stones on the left while also looking towards the centre.","30":"White 30 (H4) extends along the bottom, pressing Black’s bottom-left group while building White’s own area.","31":"Black 31 (O3) approaches White’s bottom-right corner.","33":"Black 33 (R3) attaches to White’s corner stone. From here Black builds on the outside while White crawls along the third line at the edge.","43":"Black 43 (Q8). A thick black wall now runs down the right side. White has taken the corner and edge territory; Black has gained outside strength.","44":"White 44 (L17) steps in between Black’s stones on the top side so Black’s area does not grow too large.","50":"White 50 (R18) slips under Black’s top-right corner, aiming at the corner territory.","51":"Black 51 (P13). With the wall behind it, Black frames a large area from the right side towards the centre.","52":"White 52 (D10) jumps in between Black’s stones on the left side. Black and White stones become tangled there.","63":"Black 63 (B10) plays at the left edge so that White’s stones cannot make a base there.","65":"Black 65 (G3) also goes into White’s area on the bottom side, reducing White’s territory.","73":"Black 73 (K17) firms up Black’s top-side stones and puts pressure on White’s stone of move 44.","76":"White 76 (P11) jumps into Black’s right-side area. Whether this stone lives or dies will make a big difference to the score.","77":"Black 77 (Q11) answers from the side of its wall, pushing White out towards the centre — calm defence that also attacks, typical of Shusaku.","80":"White 80 (N10) runs into the centre, trying to escape Black’s pursuit.","85":"Black 85 (N12) keeps attacking while expanding its own territory on the top and right. Gaining profit by attacking — skilful play.","91":"Black 91 (J7) plays above White’s bottom stones, capping White’s area from the centre as well.","100":"White 100 (S17) tries something in the top-right corner to reduce Black’s corner territory.","119":"Black 119 (R17) defends the top-right corner firmly and holds off White’s attempt.","122":"White 122 (G6) goes around the outside of Black’s bottom-left stones, trying to cut them off from Black’s centre.","131":"Black 131 (M5) drives a wedge into White’s area in the middle of the bottom side.","140":"White 140 (B4) tries something in Black’s bottom-left corner. A close capturing fight begins along the left edge.","156":"White 156 (A8) captures two black stones. The fight on the left edge settles after both sides take a little.","158":"White 158 (L10). White still has stones in the centre and tries to reduce Black’s central territory.","162":"White 162 (O12) captures a black stone. Even so, Black’s right-side area remains large.","172":"White 172 (K18). From here it is the endgame, starting with the border on the top side.","186":"White 186 (T16) trims Black’s territory at the top-right edge. In the endgame, both sides play the biggest points first.","205":"Black 205 (Q18) closes off the right part of the top side and fixes the territory there.","223":"Black 223 (P10) fixes the border between Black’s right-side territory and the white stones that escaped into the centre.","233":"Black 233 (Q9) captures a white stone. Black’s right-side territory is now almost entirely safe.","239":"Black 239 (A6) captures a stone and a ko appears at the left edge. The fight over single points goes on to the very end.","260":"White 260 (T6) captures a stone, making another ko at the bottom-right edge. Black takes back at 263 (T5).","267":"The game ends after 267 moves: Black wins by 11 points. No wild adventures — Shusaku simply attacked and let his territory grow naturally, a very Shusaku-like win. It was the first of his 19 straight Castle Game victories."}},"shilin":{"t":"The Shilin Guangji game","w":"Red (name not recorded)","b":"Black (name not recorded)","ev":"The everyday encyclopedia Shilin Guangji: “a game won after giving the first move, with same-side cannons”","d":"13th century","pl":"China (Song–Yuan)","lead":"This record comes from the Shilin Guangji, an everyday encyclopedia compiled by Chen Yuanjing around the end of the Song dynasty and the start of the Yuan. It is said to be one of the oldest complete xiangqi game records that survive. It is a model game in which the stronger side lets the other move first, and in only nine moves Black’s horse traps Red’s chariot.","c":{"0":"This record is more than 700 years old, from the time between the Song and Yuan dynasties. It appears in the games chapter of the encyclopedia Shilin Guangji, and it is said to be one of the oldest complete xiangqi games we still have. The players’ names are not recorded. The title says the stronger player “gave the first move” to the weaker one, so this is a lesson showing how Red, who moves first, loses. Red is at the bottom and starts.","1":"Red moves the right cannon to the middle file. This is the Central Cannon, aimed straight at Black’s general, and still one of the strongest openings today. It shows that this opening was already played 700 years ago.","2":"Black answers with a cannon to the middle too, so the two cannons face each other. When both sides move the cannon on the same side, it is called Same Direction Cannons. Cannon against cannon has been a popular fight for centuries.","3":"Red jumps a horse out to guard the middle soldier. That soldier is under attack from Black’s cannon, so it needs a defender. The horse is also getting ready to come forward.","4":"Black does the same and guards the middle soldier with a horse. Both sides are building a firm base first.","5":"Red moves the right chariot one step sideways onto an open file. The chariot is the strongest piece on the board, so it is good to get it working early.","6":"Black brings out the other horse and leaves the chariots at home for now. The plan is to bring pieces forward first. But a chariot that comes out late can be hard to use in the attack later.","7":"Red’s chariot rushes across the river into Black’s camp. It wants to threaten Black’s pieces, but Red’s other pieces are still at home, so this attack is a bit hasty. Bringing out the left horse or chariot first was also possible.","8":"Black pushes the soldier on file 3 one step. This opens a path for the horse so it can jump forward later. Black is almost inviting Red’s chariot to come.","9":"Red’s chariot takes a soldier and now eyes the black horse beyond it. Winning a piece and threatening another looks great. But the chariot has run far ahead with no friends around it. A chariot alone in the enemy camp is easy to chase.","10":"Black’s horse jumps forward. It escapes from the chariot and moves toward Red’s camp at the same time — two jobs with one move. This horse will be the star of the game.","11":"Red pulls the chariot back one step to attack the horse from the side. A chariot can capture from far away, so the horse cannot rest. Still, this move does not help Red develop any other piece.","12":"Black’s horse goes further forward and crosses the river into Red’s camp. Each time it runs from the chariot, it gets closer to Red’s general.","13":"Red pushes a soldier to open a path for Red’s own horse. The idea is to bring that horse out and trade it for Black’s horse. But this gives Black time to go even further.","14":"Black lifts an elephant and attacks Red’s chariot. Using a defending piece to chase the enemy chariot away is very efficient. Red must move the chariot again, and Black gains another move.","15":"Red’s chariot slides across and takes another soldier. Red is now two soldiers up, but this is the move that loses. The chariot is now far from its general and cannot come back to defend. Red needed a move to guard against the horse, such as bringing the chariot home.","16":"Black’s horse jumps deeper and attacks Red’s chariot. And there is more: if the horse jumps once again, it will check Red’s general and at the same time attack the chariot in Red’s corner. One horse has made two threats at once.","17":"Red chases the horse with the chariot. But the horse can no longer be stopped. Around Red’s general there is nobody left to help.","18":"The horse leaps in with check! It also attacks the chariot in Red’s corner, so when the general steps away, the chariot will be lost. Only nine moves, but the game teaches what happens to a chariot that runs ahead alone, and why pieces should fight together. Players 700 years ago were thinking about the same things on the same board."}},"qima13":{"t":"The Thirteen-Move Horse Sacrifice (Juzhongmi)","w":"Red (name not recorded)","b":"Black (name not recorded)","ev":"Juzhongmi, Volume 1, Game 1","d":"1632 (published)","pl":"China (Ming)","lead":"This is the very first game in the Juzhongmi (“The Secret in the Tangerine”), a xiangqi book published by Zhu Jinzhen of the Ming dynasty in 1632. Red lets Black capture a horse for free, rushes in while Black is busy, and mates in thirteen moves. Under the name “The Thirteen-Move Horse Sacrifice” it is still loved almost 400 years later.","c":{"0":"This is Game 1 of the Juzhongmi, a book printed in 1632, near the end of the Ming dynasty. Together with the later Plum Blossom Manual, it is called one of the two great classics of xiangqi. This is not a real match but a model game written to teach a plan, so there are no players’ names. Red is at the bottom and moves first, and the game is over in just thirteen moves.","1":"Red moves the right cannon to the middle. This is the Central Cannon, staring straight at Black’s general. The Juzhongmi is famous for studying how to attack with this opening.","2":"Black also moves the cannon on the same side to the middle. This is Same Direction Cannons, where the cannons clash head-on. Each side is aiming at the other’s middle soldier.","3":"Red jumps a horse out to guard the middle soldier. It protects the soldier the black cannon is aiming at, and the horse starts to work too.","4":"Black also guards the middle soldier with a horse. So far both sides have the same shape.","5":"Red moves the right chariot just one step forward. Now it can run sideways next move. A chariot that moves sideways like this is called a “horizontal chariot”, and it can quickly swing to either wing.","6":"Black moves the chariot one step sideways so it can drive straight up the board. This is a “straight chariot”. Horizontal chariot against straight chariot — two different ways of using the chariot face off.","7":"Red’s horizontal chariot swings over to a file on the left. That file runs right beside Black’s advisor, where Black’s defence is thin. Red is getting ready to break in from here.","8":"Black’s straight chariot crosses the river and goes deep into Red’s camp. Black wants to fight back with an attack of its own. But it is a move that hurries to attack before defending.","9":"Red’s chariot drives deep into Black’s camp. It stands on the elephant’s “eye”, the point an elephant must pass through, so Black’s elephant cannot move. Right next to the advisor, it is now very close to the general.","10":"Black brings the other horse out to the edge. Part of the idea is to stop Red’s chariot from sliding in sideways.","11":"Here is the horse sacrifice! Red lifts the other chariot one step. This leaves the horse in the left corner with no defender, so Black’s cannon can take it for free. But in return for one horse, Red gains time to bring both chariots into the attack quickly.","12":"Black’s cannon takes Red’s horse. It looks like Black has won a piece. But this cannon is now far from home and cannot help defend. It is said that strengthening the defence by moving up an advisor, instead of capturing, was better here.","13":"Red’s cannon moves forward. Using Black’s middle cannon as a “screen” to jump over, it now aims at Black’s horse beyond it. The cannon is a strange piece that captures by jumping over exactly one piece.","14":"Black retreats the horse to escape. But that horse was the important piece guarding the middle soldier. By running away, it leaves a hole in Black’s centre. Black had other defences, but this was the worst choice.","15":"Red’s cannon takes the middle soldier — check! With Black’s horse gone, the centre was wide open. From here Red’s attack rushes forward.","16":"Black moves an advisor up to block in front of the general. This is the only good way to stop the check.","17":"Red’s second chariot arrives on the same file. Two chariots lined up on one file are extremely strong. The plan is to break into the back of Black’s camp.","18":"Black’s general steps to the side. Otherwise it would be stared at straight down the file by Red’s two chariots.","19":"Red’s front chariot takes Black’s advisor — check! This chariot will be captured by the other advisor at once, but that is part of the plan. Red throws away a chariot to smash the guards around Black’s general. What a bold move!","20":"Black takes the chariot with the advisor. In pieces, Black is now far ahead. But the general’s house is even emptier than before.","21":"Red’s last chariot moves to the general’s file — check again. Black’s general has almost no pieces left to protect it.","22":"Black puts a cannon in front of the general to block the check. Putting a piece in between like this is called interposing.","23":"Red’s chariot takes that cannon — check again. The chariot keeps capturing Black’s pieces while chasing the general into a corner.","24":"Black’s general can only go back to the middle. Every other point is covered by Red’s pieces.","25":"Finally Red’s cannon lines up in the middle, two cannons on one file — check. The front cannon is the screen and the back cannon hits the general: a “double cannon” mate. Black’s general has nowhere to go, and Red wins. By giving up just one horse, Red won in thirteen moves. For 400 years this game has been teaching that time and active pieces can matter more than the number of pieces."}},"sokei1607":{"t":"The oldest shogi record that survives","w":"Ōhashi Sōkei (I)","b":"Hon'inbō Sansa","ev":"The oldest game on record","d":"June 1607","pl":"Japan (the place is not recorded)","lead":"Of all shogi games written down move by move, this is the oldest one we still have. Sente is Sōkei, who later founded the Ōhashi house of shogi masters. Gote is Sansa, founder of the Hon'inbō house of Go, who was also a strong shogi player. Sōkei wins in 133 moves.","c":{"0":"Welcome, I am Musowl. This game was played in 1607, just as the Edo period was beginning. Sente, Ōhashi Sōkei, would later be called the first Meijin, the top shogi master of the land. Gote, Hon'inbō Sansa, founded the Hon'inbō house of Go and was very good at shogi too; the two often played in front of important people. Eight of their even games survive, and Sōkei is said to have won seven of them. More than 400 years later, we can still replay every move.","1":"P-7f. This opens the bishop's diagonal, and it is still the most common first move today. So shogi already began this way 400 years ago.","2":"P-3d. Gote opens the bishop's diagonal too. Now the two bishops face each other across the middle of the board.","3":"S-4h. Sente lifts the right silver. It is getting ready to bring the silver forward.","5":"P-4f. Sente pushes the pawn on file 4 to make room for the silver. It goes together with S-4g next.","6":"S-4b. Gote also lifts a silver. Defenders are gathering on Gote's file 4.","10":"R-4b. Gote moves the rook to file 4. Today this is called the Fourth File Rook, one of the Ranging Rook plans where the rook swings to the left. People were already playing it 400 years ago.","11":"R-4h. Sente's rook goes to file 4 too, so both rooks face each other there. Sente will move this rook many more times, looking for a good place to attack.","12":"B-3c. The bishop steps up to add strength to file 4. Gote has made file 4 very solid.","16":"K-6b. Gote moves the king to the left. In Ranging Rook, the king usually hides on the side away from the rook. The farther the king is from the fighting, the safer it is.","17":"K-6h. Sente moves the king to the left as well. It will go on to 7h and then 8g.","19":"K-7h. The king reaches file 7. The next goal is to gather golds and silvers around it to build a castle.","21":"G-5h. The right gold comes toward the king. The gold is a great defender, and having one close by makes the king feel safe.","24":"S-5c. Gote brings a silver to file 5. With silvers on 5c and 4c, the center is solid too.","29":"S-6g. Sente's silver arrives near the king. With the silver on 6g and the gold on 7h, a wall is forming around the king.","30":"G-6c. Gote lifts a gold to thicken the front of the king. Both players are building their camps slowly and carefully.","33":"P-8f. Sente pushes the pawn on file 8 to make a place for the king. It is preparing K-8g.","35":"K-8g. The king goes to 8g. It is not a castle we use today: the king sits high, near the edge. We can see how players of that time thought about defence.","36":"K-8b. Gote's king settles on 8b. Each king is now tucked away deep in its own corner.","39":"N-7g. The knight jumps up so that it can leap to 6e at any time. Sente wants the castle's pieces to help in the attack too.","41":"R-3h. Sente shifts the rook to file 3. Gote's file 4 is too strong, so Sente is changing the place to attack.","42":"R-3b. Gote at once moves its rook to file 3 as well. Wherever Sente's rook goes, Gote's rook follows.","43":"P-2e. Now Sente pushes the pawn on file 2. The plan is to bring the rook to file 2 and attack there.","44":"B-5a. Gote pulls the bishop back. It rearranges its pieces so that other pieces can meet an attack on file 2.","45":"R-2h. The rook is back on its starting file 2. File 4, file 3, file 2: Sente's rook has made a long journey.","46":"R-2b. Gote's rook again follows to file 2. Wherever Sente sends its rook, Gote meets it on the same file. This 'rook chase' is the highlight of the opening of this game.","47":"P-6e. Now Sente starts the fight on file 6, where no rook stands. Instead of attacking where the rooks stare at each other, Sente strikes where Gote is thinner. The middle game begins.","49":"Px6d. Sente pushes the pawn and takes. The idea is to lure Gote's gold forward and loosen the defence around the king.","50":"Gx6d. Gote takes back with the gold. The gold has moved a little away from its king, so a small gap opens in Gote's defence.","51":"P-4e. Next Sente pushes the pawn on file 4. It shakes Gote's defence on file 4 as well.","53":"N-3g. The other knight jumps up to join the attack. Sente's attackers are moving forward one by one.","54":"P*6e. Gote drops a pawn on 6e, the square where Sente's knight wants to jump. A pawn is cheap, but it is very useful for blocking an enemy piece's path.","56":"P-7e. Gote strikes back. It pokes at the file right above Sente's king. If the pawns above a king are broken, the king becomes hard to defend.","57":"Px7e. Sente takes with the pawn. If not, Gote's pawn would push on to 7f.","58":"Gx7e. Gote takes back with the gold, which now stands on 7e. Gote is bringing the gold forward to press on Sente's king.","59":"P*7f. Sente drops a pawn to block the gold. This pawn guards the head of the king.","61":"R-6i. Sente's rook travels all the way to the bottom of file 6. It wants to add the rook's power to the fight on file 6. Later this rook will be taken by Gote's silver.","63":"Nx6e. The knight from 7g jumps and takes the pawn on 6e. The knight Sente prepared long ago finally gets to work.","64":"Nx6e. Gote takes back with its own knight. The knights are traded.","66":"P-8e. Gote presses on file 8 too. It tries to strip away the pawns in front of Sente's king one by one.","68":"P*7g. Gote drops a pawn on 7g, right next to the king. If Sente takes it, the taking piece is pulled out of place; if not, the pawn will promote. It is a common trick to break a defence with a small pawn.","69":"G-7i. Sente steps the gold back out of the way. It chooses to keep its defensive shape instead of taking the pawn now.","70":"N*7e, check. Gote drops a knight. The knight reaches the king on 8g by jumping over other pieces. Gote's attack is getting strong.","72":"Sx7e. After Sente's pawn takes the knight, Gote takes back with the silver and moves forward. Gote's attacking pieces are now right next to Sente's king.","74":"P*8f, check. Gote drops another pawn right in front of the king on 8g. The king cannot stay there any longer.","75":"K-9h. Sente's king escapes to 9h on the edge. It steps away from danger and rebuilds its defence.","77":"Px7e. Now Sente's pawn on 7f takes the silver on 7e. Sente removes the centre of Gote's attack and gets a chance to strike back.","79":"S*7f. Sente drops a silver to clash with the gold on 7e. It guards the king's head and tries to drive the gold away.","81":"Sx7f. Silver and gold have been traded. Sente is cutting down Gote's attackers one at a time.","83":"Px6e. The pawn on 6f takes the knight on 6e. Gote loses another attacker, and Sente's pieces in hand grow.","84":"S*7h. Gote drops a silver that aims at the rook on 6i. Sente will lose the rook, but in the meantime it hurries its own attack.","85":"P*9b. Now Sente's counterattack begins. A pawn dropped on 9b, near Gote's king, pulls the lance forward. Moving the pieces around the king creates gaps.","86":"Lx9b. Gote takes the pawn with the lance. But now the lance stands on 9b, and it becomes a target for the next knight.","87":"N*8d. Sente drops a knight that attacks both the lance on 9b and the gold on 7b. Hitting two things at once is just what a knight does best.","88":"B-5a. Gote pulls the bishop back so that it aims along the diagonal at the knight on 8d. Gote wants to take the knight and stop the attack.","89":"N*7d, check. Sente drops its other knight, and it checks Gote's king. A knight can check by jumping over other pieces, so it is hard to defend against.","90":"K-8c. Gote's king steps up. Moving forward also brings it near Sente's knights.","91":"Nx9b+. The knight on 8d takes the lance and promotes, becoming a piece that moves like a gold. Sente has broken into the area near Gote's king.","92":"Kx9b. Gote takes the promoted knight with the king. Gote's king now stands on the edge at 9b.","93":"Bx7g. Sente's bishop takes the pawn on 7g. The bishop has not moved all game, but now it starts working. It will play a big part later.","94":"Sx6i+. Gote's silver takes Sente's rook and promotes. Losing a rook is a big loss, but Sente judges that its attack on the king is faster.","95":"Gx6i. Sente takes back the promoted silver with the gold. Sente's defending gold is still standing firm.","97":"P*9c, check. A pawn is cheap, but dropped right in front of a king it becomes very strong.","98":"K-8a. Gote's king retreats to 8a. Its escape routes are getting fewer.","99":"Bx8f. The bishop takes the pawn on 8f. Along its diagonal it now sees deep into Gote's camp. It is preparing the next big move.","101":"G*9b, check. The gold on 9b and the pawn on 9c work together to drive Gote's king off the edge.","103":"B-5c+. The bishop on 8f flies all the way to 5c and promotes into a horse. A horse moves like a bishop plus one step in any direction, a very strong piece. Sente's big piece has broken in close to Gote's king.","104":"K-6a. Gote's king runs on to file 6. It is being pushed from the edge toward the middle of the board.","105":"L*6d. Sente drops a lance. A lance moves straight forward as far as it likes, so it watches the whole of file 6.","106":"P*6b. Gote drops a pawn to block the lance's path.","107":"+Bx4c. The horse takes the silver on 4c. Gote loses another defender.","108":"N*5b. Gote drops a knight to strengthen its defence. The position is hard, but Gote keeps fighting.","109":"K-8g. Here Sente moves its own king up to 8g once. It seems to be giving the king more room before Gote strikes back.","110":"R*8i, check. Gote strikes back, dropping a rook to give check. Gote is trying to catch Sente's king too.","112":"P-7g+, check. The pawn promotes into a tokin and gives check. A tokin moves like a gold, yet if it is captured the opponent only gets a pawn.","114":"Rx6i+. The rook takes the gold and becomes a dragon. Sente's king has lost its defenders, and the game becomes a race: who catches the other king first?","115":"S*5c. Sente drops a silver to tighten the net around Gote's king. Pressing the enemy king even when your own king is in danger is the heart of such a race.","116":"S*6f, check. Gote drops a silver and checks. Sente's king must run.","117":"K-8f. The king escapes to 8f. Sente's king has plenty of room upward.","119":"K-9e. The king goes on to 9e at the edge. Gote's pieces can hardly reach it here. Sente's king is safe for now, so Sente can focus on attacking.","120":"P-6c. Gote pushes a pawn to defend. But Gote's defence is no longer enough.","121":"S*6b. Here Sente begins the finish. It drops a silver right into Gote's camp to take the pieces guarding the king.","122":"Bx6b. Gote takes the silver with the bishop.","123":"Sx6b+. The silver on 5c takes the bishop and promotes. Gote loses a big piece.","125":"Nx6b+. The knight on 7d takes the gold and promotes as well. Trading piece for piece, Sente strips Gote's defence bare.","126":"Kx6b. Gote takes with the king. But there are almost no defenders left around Gote's king.","127":"B*5c, check. Sente drops the bishop it just captured. Using captured pieces right away is what makes shogi so exciting.","129":"G*8d, check. The gold closes the king's escape from above.","131":"P*7c, check. Gote's king has no choice but to go back deeper.","133":"Lx6c+. The lance takes the pawn on 6c and promotes. Seeing that a piece coming to 6b next would leave the king no escape, Sansa admitted defeat here. Sōkei wins in 133 moves. The highlights were the 'rook chase' of the opening, the middle-game tug of war with pawns and knights, and the ending, where Sente gave up its rook to race at the king. It shows how the ideas of a master 400 years ago still connect to the shogi of today."}}};
